repository-code: https://github.com/xiaolin200206/durian-session-benchmark
url: https://github.com/xiaolin200206/durian-session-benchmark
abstract: >-
  Training, evaluation and audit code for a durian disease dataset
  collected in Peninsular Malaysia. The 560 images resolve into 73
  independent capture sessions; an image-level random split places
  79.6% of images in sessions that straddle the train-test boundary,
  leaves 96.7% of test images sharing a session with training, and
  inflates macro F1 by 12.2 points on average across nine
  architectures. The repository contains the partitioning utilities
  that avoid that, a script for each of the five protocol decisions
  the accompanying paper measures, and the calibration tooling behind
  its other result: that the content-based methods in common use do
  not recover capture structure from released images. On identical
  simulated image-level partitions, a near-duplicate audit of the kind
  conventionally reported alongside dataset releases flags 4.6% of
  test images where the reference grouping flags 93.9%; the two are
  reported side by side and never divided into one another, because a
  proxy that over-merges flags images the reference does not.
  Self-supervised features recover more but reach no threshold at
  which pair precision and recall are usable together. The claim is
  bounded to those representations, to single-linkage clustering, and
  to recovery rather than to any weaker notion of protective grouping.
  The calibration scripts run unchanged on any directory of
  class-organised images, including camera-trap and citizen-science
  collections.
keywords:
  - data leakage
  - grouped cross-validation
  - capture session
  - pseudoreplication
  - field image data
  - plant disease classification
  - reproducibility
  - benchmark
